      "sectionBreaks": [
        {
          "before": "Understanding electromagnetic waves also requires the coupling of the fields: a changing magnetic field is related to electric-field circulation, and a changing electric field enters the magnetic-field circulation relation.",
          "after": "Faraday's law of induction relates electric-field circulation around a closed contour to the change in magnetic flux through a surface bounded by that contour.\nIt is not a formula that directly determines the electric-field strength at every point from the local rate of change of the magnetic field."
        },
        {
          "before": "With your right thumb along the positive axis, the induced electric-field circulation is opposite to the curl of your fingers, expressing Lenz's law.\nThis direction rule assumes the illustrated axial symmetry and increasing magnetic flux; if the flux decreases instead, the circulation reverses.",
          "after": "The original textbook presents Maxwell's reasoning as a question: if a changing magnetic field is related to electric-field circulation, could a changing electric field also be related to magnetic-field circulation?\nIt describes the search for unity in natural laws as a historical motivation for the hypothesis."
        },
        {
          "before": "The illustration does not mean that a changing electric field at any point necessarily implies a nonzero magnetic field there; the general relation concerns circulation determined jointly by conduction current and changing electric flux, with spatial distribution also relevant.",
          "after": "When an ideal parallel-plate capacitor is charging, both the conduction current in the wire and the changing electric field between the plates belong in the magnetic-field circulation description (Figure 100).\nFor the same closed contour, using either a surface that crosses the wire or one that avoids the wire and extends into the plate gap must give consistent magnetic-field circulation."
        }
      ],
